### How `when` and `validations` work

The `when` section is a gate for the validation rules. It describes the situation in which the checks in `validations` must be applied. It does not validate the record by itself. For example, a rule can say: "only when the Case origin is Email, require a Contact".

The `validations` section contains the checks to execute after the `when` section is true. A `required` validation is useful for lookups: the field must be present and must contain a value. In the example below, `caseorigincode` is the standard Case Origin option set and `primarycontactid` is the Case Contact lookup. Replace `2` with the option-set value for **Email** if it is different in the target environment.

```json
{
	"when": {
		"conditions": [
			{
				"field": "caseorigincode",
				"operator": "eq",
				"value": 2
			}
		]
	},
	"otherwise": "success",
	"validations": [
		{
			"type": "required",
			"field": "primarycontactid",
			"message": "A Contact is required when the Case origin is Email."
		}
	]
}
```

In this example, a Case with origin **Email** enters `validations`; if no Contact is indicated, the validation fails and the DocSolutions configuration is skipped. A Case with any other origin does not enter `validations`, and the configuration continues because `otherwise` is `success`.

### Configuration Properties

| Property | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `when.logic` | Logical operator for the conditions: `and` or `or`. The default is `and`. |
| `when.conditions[].field` | Logical name of the Dataverse field to read from the target record. |
| `when.conditions[].operator` | Comparison operator: `eq`, `neq`, `null`, or `notnull`. |
| `when.conditions[].value` | Value used by `eq` or `neq`; it can represent text, numbers, dates, booleans, option-set values, money, or an entity reference GUID. |
| `otherwise` | Result when `when` is false: `success` (default) or `error`. |
| `otherwiseMessage` | Optional error message returned when `otherwise` is `error`. |
| `validations[].type` | Validation type: `required` or `regex`. |
| `validations[].field` | Logical name of the field to validate. |
| `validations[].message` | Optional message returned when the validation fails. |
| `validations[].parameters.pattern` | Regular expression used by a `regex` validation. |
| `validations[].parameters.ignoreCase` | Optional Boolean value for a `regex` validation. The default is `true`. |

For a `required` validation, the field must be present and must not be null or blank. A `regex` validation is skipped when the field is absent, null, or blank; when a value is present, the `pattern` parameter is required and must match the value according to the regular expression.

The JSON can be left empty when no validation is required. Invalid JSON or invalid regular-expression parameters return an error. An unsupported condition operator evaluates to false and returns the configured `otherwise` result.
